Ask the card for a 100ms ring instead of a 250ms one.

The ALSA driver hardcodes buffer_time 250ms / period_time 50ms and its
ALSA_CommandLine is an empty function -- "no options" -- so there is no
runtime lever for either. A quarter of a second is what the ring holds,
and therefore what a sample waits between being mixed and being heard.

That is latency the caller cannot see and cannot correct. bb pumps
MikMod_Update from a 10ms timer in the same tl_group that steps its
scenes, so the picture advances on the mixer's clock while the sound
comes out 250ms behind it: the music is a beat late against a demo that
was written to hit its scene changes on the beat. Upstream's blocking
write hid this by pinning the caller to the audio clock -- and froze the
animation doing it, which is the defect alsa-nonblocking-update.patch
fixes. Both halves are needed: non-blocking so the caller runs, and a
short ring so what it hears is close to what it drew.

100ms/20ms keeps the ring at five periods, so the existing bound of
ALSA_UPDATE_MAX_PERIODS is still above it and cannot be what ends a real
call. Five periods is also 100ms of slack against a caller that stalls,
which is ten of bb's timer intervals and more than two frames of the
slowest aalib render measured on a VT -- an underrun is a click, and the
existing xrun_recovery re-prepares.

Lower is possible and is not free: at 60ms the ring stops covering a
slow frame under a compositor, which trades a delay nobody can point at
for a crackle everybody can hear.

--- a/drivers/drv_alsa.c
+++ b/drivers/drv_alsa.c
@@ -252,8 +252,12 @@
 static int ALSA_Init_internal(void)
 {
 	snd_pcm_format_t pformat;
-	unsigned int btime = 250000;	/* 250ms */
-	unsigned int ptime = 50000;	/* 50ms */
+	/* The ring is the latency: a sample sits here between being mixed
+	   and being heard, and ALSA_Update is non-blocking, so the caller
+	   keeps it full and pays the whole depth. Five periods, small
+	   enough that a demo's picture and its music stay together. */
+	unsigned int btime = 100000;	/* 100ms */
+	unsigned int ptime = 20000;	/* 20ms */
 	snd_pcm_uframes_t psize;
 	snd_pcm_uframes_t bsize;
 	unsigned int rate, channels;
